<article class="env-lemma" id="066P">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/066P">Lemma <span data-tag="066P">15.68.19</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring of finite global dimension $d$. Then </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
every module has tor dimension $\leq d$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
a complex of $R$-modules $K^\bullet $ with $H^ i(K^\bullet ) \not= 0$ only if $i \in [a, b]$ has tor amplitude in $[a - d, b]$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
a complex of $R$-modules $K^\bullet $ has finite tor dimension if and only if $K^\bullet \in D^ b(R)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The assumption on $R$ means that every module has a finite projective resolution of length at most $d$, in particular every module has tor dimension $\leq d$. The second statement follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/066I" data-tag="066I">15.68.9</a> and the definitions. The third statement is a rephrasing of the second. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>